(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-htn-raas-inhibitors-quiz"]={"title":"Renin-Angiotensin Inhibitors & First-Line Choice Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"When is an angiotensin receptor blocker used for left ventricular dysfunction?","choices":["Only after a beta blocker fails","Only with a potassium supplement","Never, as it worsens heart failure","When an ACE inhibitor is not tolerated"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor is the default in left ventricular dysfunction; the receptor blocker is the replacement when the patient cannot tolerate it. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which statement about ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitors in pregnancy is accurate?","choices":["Contraindicated in the second and third trimesters","Safe once the first trimester has ended","Contraindicated in the first trimester only","Preferred for hypertension throughout pregnancy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. They cause fetal morbidity and mortality, including birth defects and fetal death, so they are contraindicated in the second and third trimesters. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which of these drugs is an angiotensin receptor blocker?","choices":["Benazepril","Valsartan","Nisoldipine","Nadolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The -sartan suffix identifies an angiotensin receptor blocker, the same family as losartan, candesartan, irbesartan and telmisartan. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 52-year-old with type 2 diabetes and newly diagnosed hypertension needs a first agent. Which class is preferred for its kidney-protective effect?","choices":["A beta blocker","An alpha-1 blocker","An ACE inhibitor","A direct vasodilator"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitors are preferred in diabetics because they lower glomerular pressure and slow diabetic nephropathy; an angiotensin receptor blocker is the equivalent choice. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"How do angiotensin receptor blockers lower blood pressure?","choices":["By inhibiting renin release","By blocking AT1 receptors","By inhibiting the converting enzyme","By stimulating central alpha-2 sites"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. They bind the AT1 (angiotensin II type 1) receptor with high affinity and dissociate slowly, giving sustained blockade: vasodilation, less aldosterone and less sodium reabsorption. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Blood pressure has not improved after two titration steps. What is the most common cause of a lack of response?","choices":["Medication nonadherence","The white coat effect","Tolerance to the drug","Excess dietary potassium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nonadherence is listed first, ahead of the white coat effect and improper measurement, so it is considered before assuming the drugs have failed. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"What should a patient starting benazepril be told about potassium?","choices":["Take a daily potassium supplement to replace losses","Potassium is not affected by this medicine","Expect low potassium, so watch for cramps","Avoid potassium supplements and salt substitutes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitors raise potassium, and supplements or potassium-based salt substitutes add to it; renal disease raises the risk further. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor has a short half-life, making it the exception to once-daily dosing?","choices":["Lisinopril","Ramipril","Captopril","Trandolapril"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Captopril's short half-life sets it apart; most other ACE inhibitors are given once daily, though some lose effect near the end of the interval. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A patient with salt-sensitive hypertension responds poorly to losartan. What improves the response?","choices":["Doubling the losartan dose","Adding an ACE inhibitor","Adding a potassium supplement","Adding a diuretic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Angiotensin receptor blockers are less effective in the salt-sensitive form; adding a diuretic improves outcomes, which is why most come as combination products. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"In a patient with renal disease whose renal blood flow depends on angiotensin II, what can happen when an ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor is started?","choices":["Filtration rate rises sharply","Filtration rate drops sharply","Renal blood flow is unchanged","Potassium falls sharply"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Where renal blood flow depends on angiotensin II, ACE inhibitors can dramatically decrease the glomerular filtration rate, so they are used cautiously and titrated slowly. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What benefit do ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitors provide when given after a myocardial infarction?","choices":["Less remodeling, lower mortality","Dissolution of the thrombus","Faster heart rate recovery","Prevention of reflex tachycardia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. After infarction they reduce myocardial remodeling and lower overall mortality; angiotensin II otherwise drives hypertrophy and remodeling. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 64-year-old with chronic kidney disease who uses a salt substitute is started on lisinopril. Which laboratory abnormality is most important to anticipate?","choices":["Hypokalemia","Hypercalcemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypoglycemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hyperkalemia is most often seen with renal disease and in those taking potassium-sparing diuretics, potassium supplements or salt substitutes; this patient has two of those. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"When should a patient starting an ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor be most alert for angioedema?","choices":["During the first week","After six months or more","Only after a dose increase","Only after stopping the drug"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Angioedema usually develops in the first week of therapy and is reversible if the drug is removed. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How should an ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor be started in a patient with renal disease?","choices":["Full dose, reduced if needed","A loading dose, then daily doses","Low dose, increased slowly","It is never used in renal disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Because glomerular filtration can fall dramatically where renal blood flow depends on angiotensin II, the drug is used cautiously: low doses moved upward slowly. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"After an antihypertensive is started or titrated, when should blood pressure control be reassessed?","choices":["About 3 days later","About 6 months later","Only at the annual visit","About 4 weeks later"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Control is reassessed about 4 weeks after starting or titrating, and one or two titration steps are appropriate before modifying or adding medication. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"Which action belongs to angiotensin II?","choices":["Stimulating renin release","Stimulating aldosterone secretion","Dilating renal arterioles","Suppressing thirst centrally"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Angiotensin II also constricts arterioles and renal vessels, raises sympathetic outflow, and stimulates thirst and antidiuretic hormone release. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"At diagnosis, a patient's systolic pressure is 24 mmHg above goal. Which starting regimen is recommended?","choices":["ACE inhibitor plus a receptor blocker","ACE inhibitor plus a dihydropyridine","Beta blocker plus clonidine","A dihydropyridine by itself"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. When systolic pressure is more than 20 mmHg (or diastolic more than 10 mmHg) above goal, start an ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor or angiotensin receptor blocker plus a dihydropyridine. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"A patient on enalapril has a bothersome dry cough but well-controlled blood pressure. Which change is most appropriate?","choices":["Switch to an angiotensin receptor blocker","Switch to a different ACE inhibitor","Halve the dose of enalapril","Add a beta blocker to suppress it"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Angiotensin receptor blockers do not affect bradykinin metabolism, so they do not cause cough, and they keep the benefit of blocking angiotensin II. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 30"}]};
